Throughout the past five weeks, developing my Creative Business Management System has been both a challenging and rewarding learning experience. At the beginning of the course, I underestimated the scope of the project and initially believed I would have the time to be able to complete each week’s tasks on the weekends, along with a large portion of my other schoolwork. As I progressed through the course, I realized that the amount of time it would take could not fit into one weekend each week. Although this realization meant that I was behind schedule for a portion of the project, it also motivated me to improve my time management skills and dedicate additional effort to completing each phase of development.
During Week 1, I focused on gathering requirements and identifying the overall purpose of the application. I created functional and non-functional requirements and began considering how different users would interact with the system. This stage helped me understand the importance of thoroughly planning a project before beginning development. I also learned how clearly defined requirements can reduce confusion later in the software development process.
In Week 2, I transitioned into the design phase by creating a site map, wireframes, and several system diagrams. These visual representations helped me organize the structure of the application before writing code. I also planned the database structure and considered how information such as workshops, users, and registrations would be stored. While creating these diagrams required additional time, they made the implementation phase much easier because I already had a roadmap for how the application should function.
Weeks 3 and 4 were dedicated primarily to development. During this time, I created the application's front-end pages using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript while also implementing the back-end functionality with Python and Flask. I developed user authentication, workshop registration, administrative features, and database integration using SQLite. Throughout this phase I encountered several technical challenges, including debugging code, connecting the application to the database, resolving routing issues, and ensuring that all pages worked together correctly. Although troubleshooting sometimes became frustrating, each problem I solved improved my understanding of web application development and strengthened my confidence in programming.
The final week focused on testing, refining the application, and completing the required project documentation. I reviewed each feature to ensure it functioned as intended and corrected errors that were discovered during testing. I also completed the Software Requirements Specification, project report, and supporting documentation. This stage reinforced the importance of documentation in software development because it allows future developers and stakeholders to understand both the design decisions and the implementation of the system.
One of my greatest successes during this project was successfully combining multiple technologies into one cohesive application. Before this course, I had limited experience developing a complete web application that integrated front-end design, back-end programming, routing, user authentication, and a relational database. By the end of the project, I gained valuable experience using HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Python, Flask, SQLite, and GitHub together within a single project. Seeing the finished application function as intended was extremely rewarding and demonstrated how much I had learned throughout the course.
My biggest challenge was managing my time effectively. This experience taught me the importance of carefully reviewing assignment requirements early and breaking large projects into smaller, manageable tasks. In future projects, I will begin implementation earlier and dedicate consistent time each week to avoid falling behind.
If I were offering advice to future students completing a similar project, I would recommend beginning the planning documents as early as possible and treating them as valuable development tools rather than simply assignment requirements. Creating wireframes, diagrams, and detailed requirements before writing code saves considerable time later in the project. I would also encourage students to test their application frequently instead of waiting until the end. Identifying and correcting problems incrementally makes debugging much easier than trying to fix numerous issues all at once. Finally, I would recommend using version control consistently and making regular backups to avoid losing progress.
Overall, this project was one of the most comprehensive software development experiences I have completed. It challenged me to apply concepts from multiple areas of web development while reinforcing the complete Software Development Life Cycle from planning through implementation and testing. Although there were moments of frustration, overcoming those obstacles ultimately made the experience more valuable. I leave this first project with a much stronger understanding of software design, web application development, and documentation, and I feel better prepared for future programming and software development projects.

